  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nRadar trends early this morning highlights leftover showers from\nlast night slowly petering out across the southern coalfields,\nwith isolated showers sprouting around the forecast area amid\nblanketed stratus. The latest surface analysis places the cold\nfront directly over the forecast area this morning, which will\ncontinue to serve up low ceilings and patchy fog through the\npredawn hours before it begins to retreat northward as a warm\nfront.\n\nPrecipitation chances will become focused along the higher\nterrain this afternoon and further northward in Ohio and\nPennsylvania where the warm front will be draped. The severe\nrisk for this afternoon will be primarily aligned along the\nboundary, but a marginal risk clips portions of Perry County for\nthe potential of isolated severe wind gusts. Hydro concerns will\nbe geared towards the mountains and Greenbrier Valley, where\nheavier downpours could lead to isolated high water issues.\n\nOtherwise, the Charleston metro and Tri-State areas and down\ninto the southwestern coalfields will see a lull in\nprecipitation today with the departure of the warm front.\nTemperatures in these locations are likely to reach the upper\n70s to low 80s this afternoon, while our northern and mountain\nzones will be capped in the 60s to low 70s due to lingering\ncloud cover and afternoon shower/storm potential.\n\nTurning quieter tonight with mountain showers and storms coming\nto an end. An encroaching low pressure system slated to arrive\nfor the end of the work week will plant the CWA in the warm \nsector overnight tonight, with lows hovering in the mid to upper\n60s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nLow pressure strengthening off of the lee of the Rockies on\nThursday will drive a strong cold front through the Ohio Valley\nand Central Appalachians for the end of the work week. The\nforecast remains on track with a line of showers and storms\nattached to the front dashing across the forecast area Friday\nafternoon into the evening. A tightening pressure gradient\ndeveloping behind the front will yield gusty winds by Friday\nevening, especially along the higher terrain, where forecast\nwind gusts could reach strengths of 30 to 40 miles per hour.\n\nPost-frontal northwesterly flow will open the door for sub-\nfreezing temperatures and lake enhanced moisture to infiltrate\nthe area overnight into Saturday morning. This combination will\nadvertise a brief period of wintry precipitation along the\nhigher terrain Saturday morning as lingering showers become\nsuppressed over the Appalachians. Accumulations remain unlikely\nwith this event for snow, but a few flakes could transpire along\nour higher mountain zones.\n\nPrecipitation chances wrap up by the second half of the day\nSaturday as high pressure builds in from the west. This will\nhelp to soften the grips of the pressure gradient and cause\ngusty surface winds to gradually peter out by the afternoon.\nLow level wind flow remains out of the northwest for the rest of\nthe weekend, encouraging arctic air to continue pressing over\nthe area. This will yield weekend morning lows to drop below\nfreezing, especially on Sunday when a widespread freeze is\nanticipated. A moderating trend slowly arises heading into next\nweek as the surface high propagates to the east and winds veer\nback out of the south.",
